plan Testing
title   Deterministic Stories, checkpoints, models and property tests
status  proposed
goal
    App authors reproduce application behaviour with isolated Stories, inspect faithful captured
    state and replay generated failures against the production Runtime.
outcomes
    O1  Stories
        story.app(setup, { id, fixtures? }) accepts a typed RuntimeSetup. story.machine(machine, {
        id, input?, context?, services?, fixtures? }) tests one fresh actor. Focused input and
        context are required exactly when declared and have their declared types; services must
        satisfy the machine's requirements. App Stories exercise real App wiring. A Story id is
        nonempty and unique within its App; derived exported Stories need distinct ids.

        Construction, import and discovery start no services, actors, fixtures or assertions.
        Commands return new static plans without changing earlier prefixes. Branches replay in fresh
        Runtimes. Scenario commands accept no arbitrary callbacks, conditional branches, live
        handles or raw state overrides. run({ signal? }) is the execution boundary and returns a
        Promise of a StoryRun containing checkpoints, end, trace and any captured payloads.

        Both Story kinds support send, process, advance, advanceTo, advanceToNextTimer, checkpoint
        and run. App ensure(ref, options?) follows @plan.Runtime.O3 at the chosen step. App
        send(ref, event) targets that exact ref; focused send(event) targets its actor. send never
        creates an absent actor. Only public events can be sent; internal events require their
        declared producers. Focused setContext(context) replaces the actor's context as a production
        context update: a changed value runs onContext as an ordinary turn, an equal value is
        silent, and the initial context is a silent baseline (@plan.Runtime.O5). App Stories cannot
        inject context.

        Checkpoint names retain exact literal or known-union keys. Duplicate names, widened strings
        and access through unknown literal keys are type errors; other commands preserve the keys.
        defineConfig({ apps: [{ app, stories }] }) discovers configured exported Stories and rejects
        duplicate or empty ids, foreign setups, incompatible machines and lookalike package values
        before acquiring resources. Loading and configuration discovery follow @plan.CommandLine.O6.
    O2  Explicit processing and time
        Identical plans, seeds and fixture inputs produce identical ordered traces. Each run has
        fresh service state, seeded ids and randomness (each tab seeded from the run seed, tab name
        and open count, so ids never repeat across reloads), and a controlled clock; host time and
        randomness never choose scenario order. Stories execute production behaviour.

        process() drains currently eligible work that can settle without moving time. It waits
        neither for an open stream to end nor for deliberately blocked external input. Pending work
        remains visible. It never invents results, advances time or executes another run's work.
        Each call permits 10,000 production turns. The attempted 10,001st turn rejects with
        StoryTurnLimit before executing; later commands do not run and partial evidence survives.
        There is no maxTurns option. advance(duration) accepts a finite nonnegative Duration.Input.
        advanceTo(epochMilliseconds) accepts a finite safe integer at or after now.
        advanceToNextTimer() moves to the earliest pending deadline, stays at now when already due,
        and does nothing when no timer exists. These commands make work eligible without draining
        it; process() applies the due work. Invalid or backwards movement rejects with
        InvalidDuration before changing time.
    O3  Fixtures
        Fixtures are Effect Layers replacing whole service functions while preserving their input,
        success, failure, cancellation and lifetime contracts. Delays and responses pass through
        real services and the controlled clock; fixtures cannot inject operation outcomes directly.
        Fixtures may depend on setup services and shadow those services. Duplicate service Tags
        among fixtures reject with DuplicateFixture before either conflicting provider is acquired;
        replacement requires an explicit overriding form identifying the replaced fixture and
        replacement Layer. Fixtures providing no service are valid. Missing service requirements are
        type errors; untyped callers fail before actor activation.

        Every run has fresh scoped providers and server truth. Stateful fixtures remember writes and
        idempotency results and update that truth before emitting pushes. Story persistence always
        uses a fresh in-memory KeyValueStore; setup and fixtures never open external storage or
        inherit a live store. Storage fixtures may seed encoded records before bootstrap, but cannot
        bypass production validation. Acquisition failures still trigger @O8 cleanup.
    O4  Faithful checkpoints
        checkpoint(name) captures immediately without processing. end captures after the last
        command, before cleanup; it does not mean actors have reached final states. A cut includes
        every live actor in that Runtime, including local and nonpersisted actors, and no foreign
        actor. It captures complete published snapshots, one store revision, now, pending work and
        due times, issues and evidence; partially published context waves never appear as complete.

        checkpoint.actor(ref) returns undefined for an absent actor, otherwise exposes snapshot.
        Focused checkpoints also expose snapshot directly. end supports the same reads. now is
        captured epoch milliseconds; pending identifies owners, operations or schedules, attempts
        where applicable, and deadlines. descriptor.get(checkpoint, input),
        Domain.entry.get(checkpoint, input), Entity.get(checkpoint, key, shape) and
        Entity.queue(checkpoint, key) read the captured revision without live lookups. Entity.get
        returns undefined for absence, otherwise { data, pending, uncertain, unconfirmed }; shape
        selects fields and missing fields remain missing. Inactive domain answers return undefined.
        Field and queue meanings follow @plan.EntityDomains.O2; descriptor lanes follow
        @plan.Operations.O6.

        Cuts retain cumulative repairs, skips and diagnostics in accepted order; run.trace also
        includes cleanup events. Truncation is explicit and cannot prove an absent cause, following
        @plan.Inspection.O4. Earlier evidence stays unchanged as execution continues. In-process
        snapshots retain sensitive values faithfully; application opaque values retain identity.
        Reads start no work and cannot resume a Runtime. Exported evidence follows
        @plan.Inspection.O5. Failed capture fabricates no end and preserves earlier checkpoints.

        CheckpointProvider renders exactly the captured actors and store without attachment,
        restoration or operation starts. It records attempted sends rather than executing them.
        Eligibility uses production checks, including denial reasons and context.
    O5  Models and replay
        model(baseStory, { stateKey }) accepts a command-empty focused Story, preserving its input,
        context, services and fixtures. stateKey maps a predicted snapshot to all guard-relevant
        facts; equal keys identify the same explored state. Invalid keys or callback defects throw
        synchronously with their cause and no partial result; object identity is never a fallback.

        getShortestPaths({ events, maxDepth?, limit? }) returns first shortest paths in candidate
        order. getSimplePaths uses candidate-order depth-first paths without repeated keys. Both
        include the zero-step path and return { paths, truncated, explored }; explored counts
        distinct keys including the initial key. truncated means a bound hid a reachable candidate.
        maxDepth defaults to 20 and is a nonnegative safe integer; limit defaults to 100 and is a
        positive safe integer. Invalid bounds reject synchronously. Events are local to each call.
        Exploration starts no Runtime, service, fixture or operation and moves no time. Declared
        operation outcomes and schedules may be candidates. Returned Stories obtain them through
        real fixtures or schedules, never by externally sending internal events. An unavailable
        fixture outcome fails replay parity rather than synthesizing delivery. path.state is the
        final projection; path.keyOf projects a live snapshot; path.story extends the base with the
        candidate sequence. process() remains explicit. Live replay must match predicted keys at
        every prefix and the end; pure exploration alone proves no effect behaviour.
    O6  Reproducible property tests
        propertyTest(domains, options) generates ordinary Stories on fresh production Runtimes.
        Overlapping domains are tested together, with probe subscriptions on every read entry.
        Domain properties, quietness and oracle comparisons belong to @plan.EntityDomains.O4.
        options are fixture, inputs and seed, with optional runs, steps and quietWithin. fixture is
        a stateful fixture whose server truth is the oracle. inputs keys read and write entries;
        each listed entry needs a typed generator, and unlisted entries are not exercised. Foreign
        or invalid inputs reject before the generated operation starts.

        Generators are nonempty readonly sample arrays or pure { sample, shrink } objects using the
        supplied seeded random source. Array samples are uniform and shrink toward earlier entries;
        custom shrink returns a finite ordered sequence. seed is a safe integer; runs and steps are
        positive safe integers, defaulting to 50 and 40. quietWithin defaults to 60 seconds of
        virtual time and accepts a finite nonnegative Duration.Input. Pending work at that horizon
        is inconclusive, never pass. Generated faults hold, release, fail, delay, reorder, duplicate
        or lose responses; change server truth and push; disconnect and reconnect; change
        subscriptions; invalidate; crash and restore; or adopt a supplied domain. They run through
        real service and lifecycle paths, so a lost response can leave a committed write unknown.
        The oracle reads fixture server truth directly, without cache, prediction, network faults or
        adapter calls.

        Every committed publication is checked, even when process() drains many turns. After the
        generated actions, processing and timer advances seek quietness within quietWithin. Results
        distinguish Pass, Fail and Inconclusive and retain seed, completed runs and evidence. Fail
        identifies the property and step/publication; turn exhaustion, pending external work, a
        quiet horizon or insufficient evidence is Inconclusive. Acquisition, generator and cleanup
        defects reject as execution errors rather than domain counterexamples. Shrinking
        deterministically tries action chunks, individual actions, then smaller inputs, preserving
        dependencies and input validity. Each candidate runs fresh and is retained only if the same
        property fails. Stop at 1,000 candidates or no smaller failure; report the bound. Re-run the
        final failure and emit a static Story with literal inputs, controls and seed. Repeated
        replay produces identical traces. Export redaction identifies omissions that prevent the
        printed projection alone from reproducing the failure.
    O7  Isolation, hydration and lazy features
        Run, principal and request isolation follows @plan.Runtime.O7. Tests use no browser storage
        or live state from another run.

        App Stories model reload and a second tab as tabs: independent Runtimes of the Story's setup
        sharing that run's in-memory storage. openTab(name) constructs and readies one;
        closeTab(name) ends it abruptly, with no final flush, like a closed or crashed page, and
        releases its scope ownership. send, ensure and checkpoint reads take an optional tab
        (default "main"); checkpoints expose tabs.<name>. A tab opened while another holds the scope
        runs without persistence (@plan.Persistence.O7). Tabs share nothing else; there is no
        cross-tab coordination.

        App dehydrate(label, { scope, within, maxBytes? }) captures through production server
        settle, advancing controlled time through delays until quiet or within. It stores
        run.payloads[label] and leaves the scenario Runtime available. Labels follow checkpoint key
        rules. hydrate(source, label?) accepts a payload or an independently executed source Story.
        Leading hydrate runs before bootstrap and storage restore; later hydrate models navigation.
        Payload contents follow @plan.ServerRendering.O3, validation and installation
        @plan.ServerRendering.O4, precedence @plan.ServerRendering.O5 and isolation
        @plan.ServerRendering.O6. Source cycles and missing labels reject before execution; failures
        identify the source or destination boundary. Checkpoints expose hydration evidence and
        serverView(label) for first-render comparison. dehydrate is the explicit exception to
        process-only draining.

        Lazy ensure and focused construction adopt supplied definitions at the chosen step without
        moving time or invoking a module loader, following @plan.LazyFeatures.O2. Checkpoints expose
        adopted entries; unrelated dormant records remain dormant. Real module loading is exercised
        by production host tests.
    O8  Cancellation, cleanup and failure evidence
        Cancellation stops admission and later commands, retains partial evidence and waits for
        finalization. A blocked send settles once; no committed turn or end is fabricated. Success,
        execution failure, partial acquisition and cancellation all finalize owned resources.
        Cleanup attempts each finalizer once even when another fails; fixtures cannot suppress it.
        It runs in a fixed order: stop admission, dispose actors consumers before providers, dispose
        the Runtime, then release fixture and host scopes last. Production disposal guarantees
        follow @plan.Runtime.O6.

        Execution or cancellation failure stays primary; otherwise the first cleanup failure is
        primary. Cleanup diagnostics retain their order, and cleanup failure never yields success.
        The full Cause remains available in process through cleanup; projection follows
        @plan.Diagnostics.O3 and never serializes Cause or private recovery authority. Rejection is
        a FlowError carrying readonly Story evidence: completed checkpoints, captured end and
        payloads if any, failing command index or bootstrap/capture/cleanup boundary, primary and
        cleanup diagnostics, cancellation flag, trace and truncation, and last accepted and drained
        evidence positions. Direct and CLI runs agree on results and retained evidence.
verification
    V1  Type errors prove exact inputs, context, services and checkpoint keys.
    V2  Stories prove inert construction, branch isolation, fixture overrides and fresh storage.
    V3  Tests prove clock rules, explicit draining and rejection before turn 10,001.
    V4  Tests prove complete passive cuts, stable earlier evidence and faithful rendering.
    V5  Tests prove model ordering, bounds, truncation and live replay parity at every prefix.
    V6  Property tests prove seeded replay, validated controls, result distinctions and shrinking.
    V7  Stories prove isolated hydration evidence, exact-step lazy adoption without loading, reload
        by closeTab/openTab, and a second tab running without persistence.
    V8  Tests prove cancellation, cleanup failures and direct/CLI partial-evidence parity.
questions
    Q1  What measured quiet horizon and shrink budget should replace the initial defaults?
        Retain 60 virtual seconds and 1,000 shrink candidates until measurements justify a change; a
        budget change must never turn incomplete evidence into pass.
    Q2  Deferred to build time
        Fixture composition mechanics and override syntax. Model key encoding and limits. Model path
        descriptions and traversal metadata. Property control identifiers and literal encoding.
        Checkpoint container freezing and private name lookup. Cleanup phase implementation.
        Discovery identity encoding. Evidence artifact codecs and host coverage.
